# 0017 — Verify path-suffix resolution

**Column**: done — closed into [issue 0020](0020-validate-name-resolution-across-implementations.hmd)
**Opened**: 2026-11-24
**Related**: [the format's tracker](../../status/lang.hmd),
[the canonical implementation's tracker](../../status/hmd.hmd),
[[hmd-lang-spec#path-suffix-resolution]]

## What

The language standard now says, under *Path-suffix resolution*, that an
unqualified reference may be a single name or a sequence of path components.
During the recursive search it is matched against the ending path components
of each candidate card, so `[[physics/thermodynamics]]` and
`[[thermodynamics]]` can both reach `science/physics/thermodynamics.hmd`.
Matching is by complete path components, never by a string suffix.

The standard is the source of truth. Verify that every implementation does
what it says, implement whatever is missing, and record each discrepancy
between an implementation and the standard where it is found.

No conformance case uses a multi-component unqualified reference today. The
only one in the corpus appears in a diagnostic message, not as a reference
under test.

## Steps

1. **Check the implementations against the standard**: the canonical one, the
   TypeScript one, and the Rust crate.
2. **Add conformance cases** under `examples/conformance/cases/`, covering:
   - single-component and multi-component references;
   - matching by complete path components, never by string suffix;
   - absolute and explicit relative paths;
   - resolution relative to the referring document, not the working
     directory;
   - `/` as the separator on every platform;
   - search precedence, and the namespace boundaries, a nested vault included;
   - ambiguous matches;
   - folder notes, and references with and without a card extension.
3. **Fix every implementation** until all of them pass the new cases.

## Out of scope

The specification text itself, which is settled.

## Closed

Closed into issue 0020, which validates the whole resolution algorithm across
the three implementations; path-suffix matching is one of its acceptance
criteria. The case `resolve-path-suffix` covers what the steps above list, and
the rest of the list is covered by the other `resolve-*` cases.
